 ],
 "literal": "Even if going near the shore of the ocean where waves beat, / if they see those who know, they will remove their thirst, / people will ask for a little water; like that, / even though they are near kings, devoid of fault, / poets will go towards generous people; therefore, / I too will not say, \"What is the benefit or reward I obtained?\" / I experienced poverty, therefore thinking of you I came indeed. / Begging, saying \"Give!\", is difficult indeed; you, that / whether you grant or do not grant, O one of victorious war, / who does not run away from thrown weapons, whose valor is like cloth / pure and spread, resembling, dense and high above, / where cool, many waterfalls descend, your / great forest of Koṇkāṉam, singing is easy indeed for me.",
 "translation": "Even when walking close to the shore of the wave-beaten ocean, people will ask for a little fresh water to quench their thirst if they meet someone who knows where to find it. Likewise, even when they are in the presence of kings, poets will seek out truly generous patrons. Therefore, I will not ask, 'What is the reward I have received?' I have suffered poverty, and so, thinking of you, I have come. Begging by saying 'Give!' is indeed difficult. Whether you grant my request or not, O warrior of victorious battles who never retreats from thrown weapons! Your high mountain slopes are dense with cool, cascading waterfalls that resemble pure, spread-out white cloth. Singing of your great forest of Koṇkāṉam is indeed easy for me.",
 "modern": "அலைகள் மோதும் கடலின் கரைக்கு அருகில் சென்றாலும், தாகத்தைத் தணிக்கும் வழியை அறிந்தவர்களைக் கண்டால், மனிதர்கள் குடிக்கக் கொஞ்சம் நல்ல நீரையே கேட்பார்கள். அதுபோல, அரசர்களின் அருகில் இருந்தாலும், புலவர்கள் குற்றம் இல்லாத வள்ளல்களையே நாடிச் செல்வார்கள். அதனால், 'நான் பெற்ற பயன் என்ன? பரிசு என்ன?' என்று நான் கேட்க மாட்டேன். வறுமையால் துன்புற்றேன், ஆதலால் உன்னை நினைத்து வந்தேன். 'தாருங்கள்' என்று இரந்து கேட்பது மிகவும் அரிதானது. நீ எனக்குப் பரிசுகளைக் கொடுத்தாலும் கொடுக்காவிட்டாலும், எறியப்படும் படைகளுக்கு அஞ்சி ஓடாத ஆண்மை உடையவனே! தூய வெண்மையான ஆடை விரிந்து கிடப்பது போல, உச்சி மலையிலிருந்து குளிர்ந்த பல அருவிகள் வீழ்ந்து ஓடும் உன்னுடைய பெரிய கொண்கானக் காட்டைப் பாடுவது எனக்கு மிகவும் எளிதானது.",
 "explanation": "The poet Mōcikīraṉār addresses the chieftain Koṇkāṉaṅ kiṉāṉ, seeking his patronage. He uses a beautiful analogy: just as a traveler walking by the salty ocean still seeks a small cup of fresh water from someone who knows where to find it, poets in the courts of wealthy kings still seek out truly generous patrons. The poet states that he has come because of his poverty, but he will not beg with the words \"Give me.\" Whether the chieftain rewards him or not, the poet declares that singing the praises of the chieftain's beautiful mountain country, Koṇkāṉam, is a natural and easy joy for him. The image of the vast but undrinkable ocean contrasted with a small cup of sweet water perfectly illustrates the difference between mighty but ungenerous kings and humble but benevolent patrons. The description of the cool waterfalls cascading down the high mountains like pure white cloth spread out highlights the natural wealth and beauty of the chieftain's land, reflecting his pure and noble character.",
